---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AvifToJpg from '@/components/tools/image-convert/AvifToJpg.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="avif-to-jpg">
  <AvifToJpg client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert AVIF to JPG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your .avif files onto the box above, or click to browse.</li>
      <li>If an image has transparent areas, choose a background colour. JPEG cannot store transparency, so those areas must be filled.</li>
      <li>Leave quality at 88, or adjust it. The default is high on purpose: the source is already lossy, and a high-quality second pass keeps the two sets of artefacts from adding up.</li>
      <li>Save each JPG, or download the batch as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>It works even if your browser cannot show AVIF</h2>
    <p>
      This page tries the built-in AVIF decoder of the browser first, because it is fast. If the browser
      has none (Safari before version 16, older Firefox builds, some embedded browsers), the tool uses a
      WebAssembly build of libavif that comes with the page. In both cases, the decoding happens on your
      device and no file is uploaded.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool also works offline. When the page has loaded, you can disconnect and continue converting.
    </p>

    <h2>What you gain and what you give up</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left"></th>
            <th align="left">AVIF</th>
            <th align="left">JPEG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Opened by old software</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Almost always</td></tr>
          <tr><td>File size for the same photo</td><td>Baseline</td><td>1.5–2.5× larger</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Transparency</td><td>Yes</td><td>No; flattened on conversion</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Colour depth</td><td>Up to 12-bit, HDR capable</td><td>8-bit sRGB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Decode speed</td><td>Slower</td><td>Very fast</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Editing support</td><td>Limited plug-ins</td><td>Universal</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Keeping the second encode invisible</h2>
    <p>
      A lossy-to-lossy conversion can add artefacts on top of artefacts. Three things control that here:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A high default quality. At 88, MozJPEG uses enough bits to reproduce the AVIF output accurately, without coarse re-quantisation.</li>
      <li>Progressive, trellis-optimised encoding. MozJPEG makes measurably smaller files than plain libjpeg at the same quality, so a high quality setting costs less space.</li>
      <li>No resizing. The image keeps its original pixel dimensions, so nothing is resampled.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      To keep transparency or get a lossless result, use <a href="/avif-to-png">AVIF to PNG</a>. To
      convert in the other direction, see <a href="/image-to-avif">image to AVIF</a>. For mixed batches,
      use the <a href="/image-converter">universal image converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
